1
00:00:39,456 --> 00:00:42,960
<i>In the first years
of the 21st century...</i>

2
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:49,049
<i>a third world war broke out.</i>

3
00:00:51,051 --> 00:00:52,886
<i>Those of us who survived</i>

4
00:00:53,011 --> 00:00:58,225
<i>knew mankind
could never survive...</i>

5
00:01:01,311 --> 00:01:03,564
<i>a fourth.</i>

6
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:07,067
<i>That our own volatile natures</i>

7
00:01:07,234 --> 00:01:10,946
<i>could simply
no longer be risked.</i>

8
00:01:12,656 --> 00:01:16,451
<i>So we have created
a new arm of the law...</i>

9
00:01:18,829 --> 00:01:21,707
<i>the Grammaton Cleric,</i>

10
00:01:21,832 --> 00:01:26,962
<i>whose sole task it is
to seek out and eradicate</i>

11
00:01:27,087 --> 00:01:31,633
<i>the true source
of man's inhumanity to man,</i>

12
00:01:34,886 --> 00:01:38,432
<i>his ability</i>

13
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:41,268
<i>to feel.</i>

14
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:43,103
Police!

15
00:01:58,619 --> 00:02:00,704
You know what to do.

16
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:17,471
Get down!

17
00:03:09,981 --> 00:03:14,361
Cleric. Lights out.
Maybe more than a dozen inside.

18
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:17,447
When the door's down,
blow the bulbs.

19
00:03:22,536 --> 00:03:24,621
Go.

20
00:03:57,779 --> 00:03:59,906
[gunfire]

21
00:04:02,367 --> 00:04:04,453
[silence]

22
00:04:12,294 --> 00:04:14,421
[whispering]

23
00:06:19,671 --> 00:06:21,548
- This is it.
- Where?

24
00:06:23,425 --> 00:06:25,343
There.

25
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:07,177
It's real.

26
00:07:12,098 --> 00:07:14,225
Burn it.

27
00:07:54,182 --> 00:07:58,353
Why didn't you just leave it
for the Evidentiary Team to log?

28
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:06,236
They miss things sometimes.

29
00:08:06,403 --> 00:08:11,616
I thought I'd take it down
myself, get it done properly.

30
00:08:16,955 --> 00:08:20,583
How long, Preston,
until all this is gone?

31
00:08:22,168 --> 00:08:25,005
Until we've burned
every last bit of it?

32
00:08:26,798 --> 00:08:28,842
Resources are tight.

33
00:08:30,593 --> 00:08:33,346
We'll get it all eventually.

34
00:08:38,268 --> 00:08:42,105
<i>Libria, I congratulate you.</i>

35
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:48,820
<i>At last, peace reigns
in the heart of man.</i>

36
00:08:50,739 --> 00:08:53,241
<i>At last, war is but a word</i>

37
00:08:53,366 --> 00:08:56,161
<i>whose meaning fades
from our understanding.</i>

38
00:08:57,370 --> 00:09:02,751
<i>At last... we... are... home.</i>

39
00:09:11,301 --> 00:09:15,472
<i>Librians, there is a disease
in the heart of man.</i>

40
00:09:16,806 --> 00:09:19,184
<i>Its symptom is hate.</i>

41
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:23,688
<i>Its symptom is anger.</i>

42
00:09:25,231 --> 00:09:27,734
<i>Its symptom is rage.</i>

43
00:09:29,736 --> 00:09:32,572
<i>Its symptom is war.</i>

44
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:39,412
<i>The disease is human emotion.</i>

45
00:09:40,914 --> 00:09:44,959
<i>But Libria, I congratulate you.</i>

46
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:47,420
<i>For there is a cure
for this disease.</i>

47
00:09:47,587 --> 00:09:51,257
<i>At the cost of the dizzying
highs of human emotion,</i>

48
00:09:51,424 --> 00:09:55,136
<i>we have suppressed
its abysmal lows.</i>

49
00:09:55,261 --> 00:10:00,809
<i>And you, as a society,
have embraced this cure.</i>

50
00:10:02,227 --> 00:10:04,395
<i>Prozium.</i>

51
00:10:04,562 --> 00:10:08,316
<i>Now we are at peace
with ourselves</i>

52
00:10:08,441 --> 00:10:11,569
<i>and humankind is one.</i>

53
00:10:11,694 --> 00:10:16,116
<i>War has gone. Hate, a memory.</i>

54
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:22,122
<i>We are our own conscience now
and it is this conscience</i>

55
00:10:22,288 --> 00:10:26,918
<i>that guides us to rate EC-10
for emotional content</i>

56
00:10:27,043 --> 00:10:31,131
<i>all those things that might
tempt us to feel again</i>

57
00:10:31,297 --> 00:10:32,841
<i>and destroy them!</i>

58
00:10:32,966 --> 00:10:37,345
<i>Librians, you have won!</i>

59
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:40,974
<i>Against all odds
and your own natures,</i>

60
00:10:42,058 --> 00:10:45,395
<i>you have survived.</i>

61
00:11:00,034 --> 00:11:02,078
<i>[bleeping]</i>

62
00:11:06,583 --> 00:11:09,419
Every time we come from
the Nethers to the city,

63
00:11:09,544 --> 00:11:12,672
it reminds me
of why we do what we do.

64
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:14,924
It does?

65
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:18,803
I beg your pardon?

66
00:11:22,807 --> 00:11:24,851
It does.

67
00:11:37,530 --> 00:11:41,284
<i>The following items have been
rated EC-10 Condemned.</i>

68
00:11:41,451 --> 00:11:44,329
<i>Seven works of two-dimensional
illustrated material,</i>

69
00:11:44,454 --> 00:11:46,873
<i>seven discs of musical content,</i>

70
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:50,752
<i>twenty interactive
strategy computer programs.</i>

71
00:11:58,635 --> 00:12:02,680
Thank you for coming, Cleric.
I assume you know who I am.

72
00:12:02,805 --> 00:12:05,391
Yes, sir, of course.
You are Vice-Council Dupont,

73
00:12:05,516 --> 00:12:09,771
of the Third Counciliary of
the Tetragrammaton. Father's voice.

74
00:12:09,938 --> 00:12:15,735
I am told that you were
very nearly a prodigal student,

75
00:12:15,902 --> 00:12:20,114
knowing almost instantly
if someone is feeling.

76
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:22,242
I have a good record, sir.

77
00:12:22,367 --> 00:12:25,203
Why do you imagine that is, Cleric?

78
00:12:25,328 --> 00:12:28,414
I'm not sure, Vice-Council.

79
00:12:28,581 --> 00:12:31,501
Somehow...

80
00:12:31,626 --> 00:12:37,382
I'm able, on some level,
to sense how an offender thinks,

81
00:12:37,507 --> 00:12:42,553
to put myself in their position.

82
00:12:42,679 --> 00:12:46,766
If you had ceased your interval.

83
00:12:46,891 --> 00:12:50,103
If you were a sense offender.

84
00:12:50,228 --> 00:12:52,647
I suppose you could
say that, sir.

85
00:12:52,772 --> 00:12:56,567
- You're a family man, Cleric?
- Yes, sir. A boy and a girl.

86
00:12:56,693 --> 00:12:59,946
The boy's in the Monastery
on path to becoming a cleric.

87
00:13:00,071 --> 00:13:02,949
Good. And the mother?

88
00:13:03,116 --> 00:13:06,286
My spouse was incinerated for
sense offence four years ago.

89
00:13:06,452 --> 00:13:10,290
- By yourself?
- No, sir. By another.

90
00:13:10,456 --> 00:13:13,126
How did you feel about that?

91
00:13:15,962 --> 00:13:20,675
I'm sorry,
I don't fully understand, sir.

92
00:13:20,842 --> 00:13:22,927
How did you feel?

93
00:13:24,304 --> 00:13:26,639
I didn't feel anything.

94
00:13:26,764 --> 00:13:30,977
Really? How is it that
you came to miss it?

95
00:13:36,774 --> 00:13:38,860
I...

96
00:13:41,404 --> 00:13:44,991
I've asked myself that same
question, sir. I don't know.

97
00:13:46,868 --> 00:13:49,829
A nearly unforgivable lapse, Cleric.

98
00:13:52,290 --> 00:13:56,210
I trust you'll be more vigilant
in the future.

99
00:13:58,171 --> 00:13:59,672
Yes, sir.

100
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:06,637
<i>Every time we come from
the Nethers to the city,</i>

101
00:14:06,763 --> 00:14:09,849
<i>it reminds me
of why we do what we do.</i>

102
00:14:10,016 --> 00:14:12,310
<i>It does?</i>

103
00:14:12,435 --> 00:14:14,687
<i>Every time we come from
the Nethers to the city,</i>

104
00:14:14,854 --> 00:14:17,857
<i>it reminds me
of why we do what we do.</i>

105
00:14:17,982 --> 00:14:19,150
<i>It does?</i>

106
00:14:21,986 --> 00:14:26,032
<i>It does.</i>

107
00:14:26,157 --> 00:14:29,911
Prosecutorial evidence
for A&R 136890.

108
00:14:30,078 --> 00:14:33,581
I need it.
It was late this afternoon.

109
00:14:33,706 --> 00:14:37,960
May not have showed up
in the records yet.

110
00:14:38,086 --> 00:14:43,007
Nothing logged and nothing
pending under that entry.

111
00:14:43,174 --> 00:14:46,552
It was an item brought in
by Grammaton Erroll Partridge.

112
00:14:46,677 --> 00:14:48,554
Check again.

113
00:14:48,721 --> 00:14:52,392
Cleric Partridge has not
entered anything in for weeks.

114
00:14:52,558 --> 00:14:55,061
You're mistaken.
It was a book of some kind.

115
00:14:55,228 --> 00:14:56,938
Cleric.

116
00:14:58,898 --> 00:15:00,942
There's nothing.

117
00:15:01,943 --> 00:15:04,028
Thank you.

118
00:15:08,032 --> 00:15:10,827
He's been into the Nether
every night for two weeks.

119
00:15:10,952 --> 00:15:14,372
We assumed
it was enforcement-related.

120
00:15:56,581 --> 00:15:58,332
You always knew.

121
00:16:08,593 --> 00:16:14,348
"But I, being poor,
have only my dreams."

122
00:16:14,474 --> 00:16:18,853
"I've spread my dreams
under your feet."

123
00:16:18,978 --> 00:16:23,357
"Tread softly,
because you tread on my dreams."

124
00:16:26,652 --> 00:16:30,156
I assume you dream, Preston.

125
00:16:30,323 --> 00:16:35,119
I'll do what I can
to see they go easy on you.

126
00:16:35,244 --> 00:16:40,082
We both know they never go easy.

127
00:16:40,208 --> 00:16:43,878
- Then I'm sorry.
- No, you're not.

128
00:16:44,003 --> 00:16:47,006
You don't even know the meaning.

129
00:16:47,173 --> 00:16:51,594
It's just a vestigial word
for a feeling you've never felt.

130
00:16:53,679 --> 00:16:58,267
Don't you see, Preston?
It's gone.

131
00:16:58,434 --> 00:17:01,687
Everything that makes us
what we are, traded away.

132
00:17:01,812 --> 00:17:05,525
There's no war. No murder.

133
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:11,948
- What is it you think we do?
- No. You've been with me.

134
00:17:12,073 --> 00:17:15,910
You've seen how it can be,
the jealousy, the rage.

135
00:17:18,621 --> 00:17:20,706
A heavy cost.

136
00:17:23,209 --> 00:17:24,794
I pay it gladly.

137
00:17:48,693 --> 00:17:50,736
Don't.

138
00:18:32,987 --> 00:18:35,364
You and your partner were close?

139
00:18:41,829 --> 00:18:43,664
I hope you're as pleased
to be assigned me

140
00:18:43,789 --> 00:18:46,876
as I am the placement.

141
00:18:47,001 --> 00:18:50,338
I was told this would be
a career-making advancement.

142
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:59,847
I'm like you, Cleric. Intuitive.

143
00:18:59,972 --> 00:19:04,810
Sometimes I know what a person's
feeling before they know it.

144
00:19:14,153 --> 00:19:17,531
Cleric.

145
00:19:17,698 --> 00:19:20,326
I could only hope
one day to be as...

146
00:19:20,493 --> 00:19:23,996
uncompromising as you.

147
00:19:24,121 --> 00:19:26,165
Good night.

148
00:19:28,501 --> 00:19:30,836
<i>The later 20th century</i>

149
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:33,673
<i>saw the fortuitous
and simultaneous rise</i>

150
00:19:33,839 --> 00:19:39,178
<i>of two synergistic political
and psychological sciences.</i>

151
00:19:39,345 --> 00:19:43,516
<i>The first, the revolutionary
precept of the hate crime.</i>

152
00:19:45,518 --> 00:19:47,561
- John.
- Yes.

153
00:19:49,188 --> 00:19:51,982
I saw Bobbie Taylor
crying today.

154
00:19:52,108 --> 00:19:54,485
He didn't know, but I saw.

155
00:19:54,610 --> 00:19:58,072
Do you think I should report him?

156
00:20:03,035 --> 00:20:04,578
Unquestionably.

157
00:20:10,251 --> 00:20:15,089
<i>...the single inescapable fact
that mankind united</i>

158
00:20:15,214 --> 00:20:19,510
<i>with infinitely greater purpose
in pursuit of war</i>

159
00:20:19,677 --> 00:20:23,139
<i>than he ever did
in pursuit of peace.</i>

160
00:20:32,022 --> 00:20:36,277
<i>[Partridge] "But I, being poor,
have only my dreams."</i>

161
00:20:37,945 --> 00:20:42,408
<i>"I've spread my dreams
under your feet."</i>

162
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:46,912
<i>"Tread softly, because
you tread on my dreams."</i>

163
00:21:30,331 --> 00:21:33,751
Viviana Preston, don't move!
Don't move! Comply! Comply!

164
00:21:33,876 --> 00:21:36,170
- Stop where you are!
- Get down! Get down!

165
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:42,259
Don't shoot.
This is a lawful entry.

166
00:21:42,384 --> 00:21:45,971
We have a warrant for your
wife's arrest for sense offence.

167
00:22:00,402 --> 00:22:03,531
Hey! Get her!
Get her off him now!

168
00:22:04,990 --> 00:22:07,034
Remember me.

169
00:22:23,634 --> 00:22:29,265
<i>Awaken to triumph again
in the face of yet another day.</i>

170
00:22:29,390 --> 00:22:31,475
<i>Another step...</i>

171
00:23:06,886 --> 00:23:08,929
[son] What are you doing?

172
00:23:12,057 --> 00:23:15,185
I said, what are you doing?

173
00:23:15,311 --> 00:23:18,814
I accidentally dropped
my morning interval.

174
00:23:20,524 --> 00:23:23,903
I took it out
before I brushed my teeth.

175
00:23:24,028 --> 00:23:26,405
I never take it out
before I brush my teeth.

176
00:23:26,530 --> 00:23:29,033
Then you'll go by Equilibrium,

177
00:23:29,158 --> 00:23:31,535
log the loss
and get a replacement.

178
00:23:33,287 --> 00:23:36,373
Yes. Of course.

179
00:23:57,561 --> 00:24:01,649
Your new partner called.
He'll pick you up at ten for the A&R.

180
00:24:01,774 --> 00:24:05,277
I hope you don't mind.
I took the liberty of telling him

181
00:24:05,444 --> 00:24:09,949
you would be at Equilibrium
replacing your dose.

182
00:24:10,115 --> 00:24:13,202
No, of course not.
That was the right thing to do.

183
00:24:13,369 --> 00:24:16,288
[girl stirs food]

184
00:24:17,289 --> 00:24:19,583
Stop that.

185
00:24:26,590 --> 00:24:30,803
<i>Due to terrorist activity,
this site is temporarily closed.</i>

186
00:24:30,970 --> 00:24:35,808
<i>Proceed to the Equilibrium
Centre in Sector 8-A.</i>

187
00:24:38,060 --> 00:24:42,398
<i>Due to terrorist activity,
this site is temporarily closed...</i>

188
00:24:48,570 --> 00:24:50,531
[hooting]

189
00:24:54,618 --> 00:24:57,830
More than punctual, Cleric. Hop in.

190
00:25:11,468 --> 00:25:14,513
How are the lines? I'm surprised
you could get your interval

191
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:17,725
and get out so quickly.

192
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:20,561
No, they're fine today.

193
00:25:23,689 --> 00:25:26,984
Maybe I'll drop by later
and get my interval adjusted.

194
00:25:29,528 --> 00:25:32,156
You expecting resistance?

195
00:25:32,281 --> 00:25:36,660
That's something you'll find
about me. I'm a wary person.

196
00:25:36,785 --> 00:25:41,749
Cautious by nature.
Always expecting the worst.

197
00:25:44,209 --> 00:25:46,712
- You cannot do this.
- Tetragrammaton.

198
00:25:46,879 --> 00:25:49,548
There's nothing we can't do.

199
00:25:49,673 --> 00:25:53,052
How long have you been
off the dose? Look at you.

200
00:25:54,720 --> 00:25:56,764
Look at you!

201
00:26:02,895 --> 00:26:05,022
Look at you.

202
00:26:06,648 --> 00:26:08,776
The mirror's frame's illegal.
Destroy it.

203
00:27:10,879 --> 00:27:14,091
You're gonna burn it, aren't you?

204
00:27:14,258 --> 00:27:15,425
Eventually.

205
00:27:16,677 --> 00:27:20,305
However, you couldn't have
accumulated this by yourself.

206
00:27:20,430 --> 00:27:22,474
It'll all be sorted and examined.

207
00:27:22,641 --> 00:27:25,811
We'll discover
who your confederates are.

208
00:27:46,748 --> 00:27:48,834
We need her.

209
00:28:14,276 --> 00:28:18,363
- What's your name?
- O'Brien. Mary.

210
00:28:19,198 --> 00:28:23,035
Well, Mary, you can either wait

211
00:28:23,202 --> 00:28:27,664
and tell the Technicians
of the Palace of Justice

212
00:28:27,789 --> 00:28:30,375
or you can tell me now.

213
00:28:30,500 --> 00:28:32,544
Who are your friends?

214
00:28:36,215 --> 00:28:38,467
I'm wondering
if you have any idea at all

215
00:28:38,592 --> 00:28:41,553
what that word means - friend.

216
00:28:45,057 --> 00:28:47,559
There's nothing you don't feel?

217
00:28:49,436 --> 00:28:52,231
How about guilt?

218
00:28:52,397 --> 00:28:54,316
Let me ask you something.

219
00:29:02,908 --> 00:29:05,035
Why are you alive?

220
00:29:10,207 --> 00:29:12,251
I'm alive...

221
00:29:16,380 --> 00:29:19,549
I live...

222
00:29:19,716 --> 00:29:23,095
to safeguard the continuity
of this great society,

223
00:29:23,262 --> 00:29:25,264
to serve Libria.

224
00:29:25,430 --> 00:29:28,517
It's circular. You exist
to continue your existence.

225
00:29:28,684 --> 00:29:30,769
What's the point?

226
00:29:33,105 --> 00:29:35,274
What's the point
of your existence?

227
00:29:35,399 --> 00:29:39,278
To feel. Because you've never
done it, you can never know it.

228
00:29:39,444 --> 00:29:42,447
But it's as vital as breath.

229
00:29:42,614 --> 00:29:44,825
And without it, without love,

230
00:29:44,950 --> 00:29:49,288
without anger, without sorrow,

231
00:29:49,413 --> 00:29:52,958
breath is just a clock... ticking.

232
00:29:57,421 --> 00:30:00,048
Then I have no choice
but to remand you

233
00:30:00,173 --> 00:30:03,302
to the Palace of Justice for processing.

234
00:30:04,219 --> 00:30:06,305
Processing.

235
00:30:09,141 --> 00:30:11,018
You mean execution, don't you?

236
00:30:13,729 --> 00:30:15,772
Processing.

237
00:30:23,613 --> 00:30:27,868
The gun katas. Through analysis
of recorded gunfights,

238
00:30:27,993 --> 00:30:30,829
the Cleric has determined
that the distribution

239
00:30:30,954 --> 00:30:33,123
of antagonists in any gun battle

240
00:30:33,248 --> 00:30:36,835
is a statistically predictable element.

241
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:39,629
The gun kata treats the gun
as a total weapon,

242
00:30:39,755 --> 00:30:43,133
each new position representing
a maximum kill zone,

243
00:30:43,258 --> 00:30:46,636
inflicting maximum damage
on the maximum number of opponents,

244
00:30:46,762 --> 00:30:49,973
while keeping the defender clear
of statistically traditional

245
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:53,352
trajectories of return fire.

246
00:30:53,518 --> 00:30:55,437
By the rote mastery of this art,

247
00:30:55,562 --> 00:30:59,066
your firing efficiency
will rise by no less than 120%.

248
00:30:59,191 --> 00:31:02,861
The difference of a 63%
increased lethal proficiency

249
00:31:03,028 --> 00:31:05,697
makes the master of the gun katas

250
00:31:05,822 --> 00:31:09,159
an adversary not to be taken lightly.

251
00:31:59,418 --> 00:32:02,421
<i>[Mary] Without love,</i>

252
00:32:02,546 --> 00:32:05,966
<i>breath is just a clock... ticking.</i>

253
00:32:12,764 --> 00:32:15,892
[rapid heartbeat]

254
00:33:47,859 --> 00:33:51,613
<i>Prozium - the great nepenthe.</i>

255
00:33:51,780 --> 00:33:56,284
<i>Opiate of our masses,
glue of our great society.</i>

256
00:33:56,409 --> 00:34:00,747
<i>Salve and salvation,
it has delivered us from pathos,</i>

257
00:34:00,872 --> 00:34:06,628
<i>from sorrow, the deepest chasms
of melancholy and hate.</i>

258
00:34:06,753 --> 00:34:10,549
<i>With it we anaesthetise grief,
annihilate jealousy,</i>

259
00:34:10,715 --> 00:34:13,510
<i>obliterate rage.</i>

260
00:34:13,635 --> 00:34:17,931
<i>Those sister impulses
toward joy, love and elation</i>

261
00:34:18,056 --> 00:34:22,769
<i>are anaesthetised in stride,
except as fair sacrifice.</i>

262
00:34:24,062 --> 00:34:29,192
<i>For we embrace Prozium
in its unifying fullness</i>

263
00:34:29,359 --> 00:34:33,196
<i>and all that it has done
to make us great.</i>

264
00:35:26,875 --> 00:35:29,044
- Good morning, Cleric.
- Good morning.

265
00:35:29,169 --> 00:35:34,382
<i>...intrinsically humans
as creatures of the earth</i>

266
00:35:34,549 --> 00:35:40,138
<i>were drawn inherently
always back to one thing: war.</i>

267
00:35:40,305 --> 00:35:43,475
<i>And thus we seek to correct
not the symptom,</i>

268
00:35:43,642 --> 00:35:46,144
<i>but the disease itself.</i>

269
00:35:46,269 --> 00:35:49,439
<i>We have sought
to shrug off individuality,</i>

270
00:35:49,564 --> 00:35:53,526
<i>replacing it with conformity.</i>

271
00:35:53,652 --> 00:35:59,658
<i>Replacing it with sameness, with unity,</i>

272
00:36:00,617 --> 00:36:05,121
<i>allowing each man, woman and child</i>

273
00:36:05,246 --> 00:36:10,710
<i>in this great society
to lead identical lives.</i>

274
00:36:12,837 --> 00:36:15,757
<i>The concept of identical
viable construction</i>

275
00:36:15,882 --> 00:36:20,929
<i>allows each of us to head
confidently into each moment...</i>

276
00:36:38,738 --> 00:36:40,323
What are you doing?

277
00:36:44,285 --> 00:36:46,079
I'm rearranging my desk.

278
00:36:47,414 --> 00:36:50,417
You didn't like the way it was before?

279
00:36:50,542 --> 00:36:54,295
I had no feelings about it.
I'm merely attempting to optimise.

280
00:36:57,424 --> 00:37:00,552
Sense offenders holed up in the Nether.

281
00:37:15,650 --> 00:37:18,737
We estimate 50 men, sir.

282
00:37:18,862 --> 00:37:22,282
Intelligence tells us
they're all fully armed.

283
00:37:22,407 --> 00:37:25,493
This is the group that bombed
the Prozium factories.

284
00:37:25,618 --> 00:37:28,872
- You clean, we'll sweep.
- Yes, sir.

285
00:37:31,833 --> 00:37:34,753
Go, go, go, go! Go!

286
00:37:52,771 --> 00:37:54,230
Cover me. I'm going in.

287
00:37:58,526 --> 00:38:00,612
Nice grab, Cleric.

288
00:40:29,844 --> 00:40:32,931
Ludwig van Beethoven.

289
00:41:31,823 --> 00:41:34,993
[Beethoven symphony starts]

290
00:42:32,717 --> 00:42:37,221
Cleric, why didn't you leave
that for the Evidentiary Team

291
00:42:37,346 --> 00:42:40,433
to collect and log?

292
00:42:45,271 --> 00:42:49,567
They miss things sometimes.
I'm gonna take it in myself,

293
00:42:49,692 --> 00:42:52,487
make sure it gets done properly.

294
00:42:56,074 --> 00:42:58,743
You know, Preston,

295
00:42:58,910 --> 00:43:01,412
if we keep burning
all this contraband,

296
00:43:01,579 --> 00:43:05,291
eventually there won't be
anything left to burn.

297
00:43:05,416 --> 00:43:07,835
What'll there be for men like us?

298
00:43:07,960 --> 00:43:11,047
Sir! We got something out back.

299
00:43:28,272 --> 00:43:32,318
They were defended by women
and children. We put them down.

300
00:43:32,443 --> 00:43:35,947
This isn't the first time
we've seen this.

301
00:43:36,114 --> 00:43:40,409
Why do they keep these animals?
Do they... Do they eat 'em?

302
00:43:43,454 --> 00:43:46,666
- What do you want me to do?
- Exterminate them, of course.

303
00:43:46,791 --> 00:43:48,751
- All right, let's do it.
- No.

304
00:43:48,876 --> 00:43:51,420
What's wrong, Cleric?

305
00:43:54,966 --> 00:43:56,300
Nothing.

306
00:44:09,689 --> 00:44:13,234
Hey! Grab it. Grab it!

307
00:44:21,409 --> 00:44:24,829
Sir, toss it back in.
I'll finish it off.

308
00:44:30,293 --> 00:44:32,253
- Give it to me, sir.
- Give it to him.

309
00:44:32,378 --> 00:44:34,672
He'll finish it.

310
00:44:40,511 --> 00:44:42,597
[dog whines]

311
00:44:47,059 --> 00:44:49,145
Wait!

312
00:44:51,439 --> 00:44:53,900
It seems to me that...

313
00:44:54,025 --> 00:44:57,945
at least some of these animals
ought to be tested for disease.

314
00:44:58,070 --> 00:45:02,200
If there's an epidemic in
the Nethers, it's best we know.

315
00:45:05,870 --> 00:45:08,539
I don't quite follow your logic, Cleric.

316
00:45:08,664 --> 00:45:12,251
They were Resistance. They could
have led us to the Underground.

317
00:45:12,376 --> 00:45:15,338
But instead of interrogation,
they were slaughtered.

318
00:45:15,463 --> 00:45:19,800
But given the circumstances,
the Father has decreed

319
00:45:19,926 --> 00:45:23,930
that there will be no more
process for sense offenders.

320
00:45:24,055 --> 00:45:27,934
They are to be shot on sight
or incinerated without trial.

321
00:45:28,059 --> 00:45:30,686
It's counter to law.
You're a member of the Council...

322
00:45:30,853 --> 00:45:32,772
It is not the will of the Council.

323
00:45:32,897 --> 00:45:37,401
It is the will of Father and he is law.

324
00:45:37,568 --> 00:45:39,987
Sir...

325
00:45:40,112 --> 00:45:43,449
without the logic of process,
is it not just mayhem?

326
00:45:43,574 --> 00:45:45,743
What we have worked so hard
to eradicate?

327
00:45:45,910 --> 00:45:48,579
You must understand, Preston,

328
00:45:48,704 --> 00:45:51,749
that while you and even I
may not always agree with it,

329
00:45:51,874 --> 00:45:54,585
it is not the message
that is important.

330
00:45:54,710 --> 00:45:59,924
It is our obedience to it.
Father's will.

331
00:46:00,091 --> 00:46:04,804
Call it faith. You have it, I assume.

332
00:46:09,684 --> 00:46:13,771
Yes. I have it.

333
00:46:13,896 --> 00:46:15,439
Good.

334
00:46:36,669 --> 00:46:41,716
Confiscated evidence X-23-T-45.
Mary O'Brien.

335
00:47:45,071 --> 00:47:49,033
Cleric John Preston passing into
the Nether. Enforcement-related.

336
00:47:51,952 --> 00:47:53,996
Thank you, sir.

337
00:48:25,236 --> 00:48:27,905
I don't know what else to do with you.

338
00:48:30,741 --> 00:48:32,993
Go on. Go on.

339
00:48:35,663 --> 00:48:37,498
Go!

340
00:48:46,882 --> 00:48:49,677
[dog yelps]

341
00:48:56,892 --> 00:48:58,978
[barks]

342
00:49:12,950 --> 00:49:16,120
Fine. But you're going back
in the trunk.

343
00:49:30,426 --> 00:49:32,470
OK.

344
00:49:34,889 --> 00:49:36,974
Here.

345
00:49:47,568 --> 00:49:51,655
Step away from the vehicle.
Step away from the vehicle.

346
00:49:51,780 --> 00:49:55,993
This is your last warning.
Step away from the vehicle.

347
00:49:56,952 --> 00:49:58,871
Identification.

348
00:49:58,996 --> 00:50:01,499
Cleric. I'm here on official...

349
00:50:01,665 --> 00:50:05,169
- Identification!
- It's in my coat.

350
00:50:05,336 --> 00:50:10,382
- Where's the coat? Where is it?
- I don't have it.

351
00:50:10,508 --> 00:50:13,886
Unidentified individuals
are subject to destruction.

352
00:50:14,011 --> 00:50:17,014
You're making a mistake. I'm
a Grammaton Cleric First Class.

353
00:50:17,139 --> 00:50:19,975
- We'll search your vehicle.
- There's nothing in it.

354
00:50:20,100 --> 00:50:22,186
Search it.

355
00:50:28,442 --> 00:50:30,861
- Clear, sir.
- Keys to the trunk.

356
00:50:31,028 --> 00:50:35,199
I'm trying to tell you I have
a predawn combustion to witness...

357
00:50:35,366 --> 00:50:37,243
This is a waste of my time.

358
00:50:37,368 --> 00:50:41,038
My name is John Preston.
I'm the highest-ranking cleric...

359
00:50:41,205 --> 00:50:42,831
Wait!

360
00:50:46,377 --> 00:50:48,212
Stand off.

361
00:50:50,297 --> 00:50:52,967
I'm sorry.
I didn't recognise you, sir.

362
00:50:55,052 --> 00:50:57,555
That's OK. Just doing your job.

363
00:50:57,721 --> 00:51:01,559
Thank you. We'll escort you back
to the gate.

364
00:51:01,725 --> 00:51:04,019
- Thanks.
- [dog yelps]

365
00:51:10,276 --> 00:51:13,988
Animals.
The Nether's full of 'em.

366
00:51:14,113 --> 00:51:16,282
Sounds like it came from your trunk.

367
00:51:16,407 --> 00:51:20,744
Impossible. Just give me my keys.
I'll be on my way.

368
00:51:26,500 --> 00:51:30,754
Captain, I'm asking you one last time.

369
00:51:30,921 --> 00:51:33,340
Don't do this.

370
00:51:34,883 --> 00:51:38,470
- Down on your knees!
- Down on your knees!

371
00:51:40,889 --> 00:51:42,766
Shoot him!

372
00:51:44,602 --> 00:51:46,145
Fuck!

373
00:51:50,774 --> 00:51:53,944
What? Shit!
Shoot him, shoot him, shoot him!

374
00:54:09,037 --> 00:54:11,081
[grunts]

375
00:54:22,968 --> 00:54:25,929
Always practising, Cleric.

376
00:54:26,096 --> 00:54:28,599
I guess that's why you're the best.

377
00:54:31,268 --> 00:54:33,645
Maybe I'm just better.

378
00:54:33,771 --> 00:54:36,523
Something on your mind?

379
00:54:38,942 --> 00:54:41,236
- Why do you ask?
- The intuitive arts.

380
00:54:41,361 --> 00:54:44,531
It's my job to know
what you're thinking.

381
00:54:54,792 --> 00:54:57,127
So, then, what am I thinking?

382
00:55:02,758 --> 00:55:05,469
About the murders
in the Nether last night.

383
00:55:11,767 --> 00:55:14,269
And if they know who did it.

384
00:55:19,274 --> 00:55:21,777
Am I close?

385
00:55:23,195 --> 00:55:25,447
So tell me, do they know?

386
00:55:34,164 --> 00:55:37,501
There are theories.
I have one of two of my own,

387
00:55:37,668 --> 00:55:40,587
but at the moment
they're premature.

388
00:55:46,009 --> 00:55:48,554
I'm glad it happened.

389
00:55:51,598 --> 00:55:54,226
- Why?
- Now Father and the Council

390
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:58,897
have to create an acceleration
in the crackdown on offenders.

391
00:55:59,022 --> 00:56:01,567
Whoever did it, all they accomplished

392
00:56:01,692 --> 00:56:04,611
was a quicker end to the Resistance.

393
00:56:13,245 --> 00:56:15,956
It's gonna be a massacre, Cleric.

394
00:56:31,972 --> 00:56:36,810
I came to tell you there's a raid
in the Nether. Sector 7.

395
00:56:39,563 --> 00:56:41,565
So get ready.

396
00:57:23,106 --> 00:57:25,192
That door behind you.

397
00:57:26,735 --> 00:57:29,196
Go.

398
00:57:29,363 --> 00:57:30,739
Go, damn you!

399
00:57:35,118 --> 00:57:37,245
Get out of here.
If you don't, you're dead.

400
00:57:37,371 --> 00:57:42,125
- He'll shoot us in the back.
- I'd shoot you in the face. Go.

401
00:57:48,298 --> 00:57:51,093
Damn you! Follow me. Now!

402
00:57:51,218 --> 00:57:52,970
Let's go.

403
00:58:07,484 --> 00:58:11,238
<i>Prisoners and apprehendees
are to be put down.</i>

404
00:58:18,912 --> 00:58:20,998
Look out!

405
00:58:33,468 --> 00:58:35,762
What's happening?
We heard gunshots.

406
00:58:37,681 --> 00:58:40,559
- Go!
- What are you doing?

407
00:58:40,684 --> 00:58:44,146
They're Resistance fighters. Cleric!

408
00:58:46,857 --> 00:58:49,192
He's a sense offender!

409
00:59:29,399 --> 00:59:32,069
[shouting]

410
00:59:46,374 --> 00:59:47,459
What is this?

411
00:59:49,336 --> 00:59:52,923
- What are you doing?
- Nicely done, Cleric.

412
00:59:53,090 --> 00:59:55,634
You drive them into the trap.

413
00:59:57,260 --> 00:59:59,554
I close it.

414
01:00:04,601 --> 01:00:07,938
The very definition of teamwork.

415
01:00:09,606 --> 01:00:11,149
Don't you think?

416
01:00:13,610 --> 01:00:16,696
Why don't you take the honours
of the execution, Cleric?

417
01:00:21,284 --> 01:00:24,788
These people should be taken
for clinical interrogation.

418
01:00:24,955 --> 01:00:27,541
Father's rulings are quite clear.

419
01:00:27,666 --> 01:00:30,710
Offenders are to be shot on sight.

420
01:00:30,836 --> 01:00:34,339
They have valuable information.
They can be put to better use...

421
01:00:34,464 --> 01:00:36,591
Cleric.

422
01:00:36,758 --> 01:00:40,512
If your weapon's low, please use mine.

423
01:01:29,769 --> 01:01:31,855
No.

424
01:01:33,356 --> 01:01:36,359
I think, in the end,

425
01:01:36,526 --> 01:01:39,863
it'll be better if you have it.

426
01:02:00,967 --> 01:02:03,678
- Captain.
- Yes, sir. Firing positions!

427
01:02:08,225 --> 01:02:10,310
Ready!

428
01:02:18,068 --> 01:02:20,237
Aim!

429
01:02:25,742 --> 01:02:27,577
Fire!

430
01:02:34,459 --> 01:02:36,920
- Sir.
- Yes, Cleric.

431
01:02:39,089 --> 01:02:43,927
You asked me to be an instrument
against the Resistance.

432
01:02:44,052 --> 01:02:46,596
I'm ready. Today.

433
01:02:48,139 --> 01:02:51,685
I wish to show my faith.

434
01:02:51,810 --> 01:02:54,062
I wish with your permission

435
01:02:54,187 --> 01:02:56,940
to locate the Underground
once and for all.

436
01:02:57,065 --> 01:02:59,109
To destroy it.

437
01:03:01,027 --> 01:03:04,322
- To destroy it.
- Good. Do it.

438
01:03:14,165 --> 01:03:18,503
I'm so... so very sorry.

439
01:03:30,432 --> 01:03:33,643
These are the possessions he had
on him at the time of death.

440
01:03:33,810 --> 01:03:35,770
The legal ones will be burned with him.

441
01:03:35,895 --> 01:03:37,647
Good.

442
01:04:18,146 --> 01:04:20,190
Erroll Partridge.

443
01:04:23,693 --> 01:04:25,695
It's supposed to mean something?

444
01:04:25,820 --> 01:04:29,240
He was a Grammaton Cleric
First Class. You knew him.

445
01:04:29,366 --> 01:04:31,951
News bulletin. I'm a sense offender.

446
01:04:32,077 --> 01:04:34,954
I don't hang round much
with the Cleric.

447
01:04:41,586 --> 01:04:43,880
I wanna know about him.

448
01:04:46,508 --> 01:04:50,261
Well, I suggest you go ask him,

449
01:04:51,721 --> 01:04:54,682
but I understand that he's dead.

450
01:04:54,808 --> 01:04:58,645
Killed by your friends
at the Tetragrammaton.

451
01:04:58,770 --> 01:05:02,941
Not by my friends. By me.

452
01:05:47,777 --> 01:05:49,028
You were lovers.

453
01:06:30,153 --> 01:06:32,655
And thus it is in the writing
of the Father

454
01:06:32,822 --> 01:06:36,367
that we find our greatest...

455
01:06:36,493 --> 01:06:38,953
Good afternoon, sir.

456
01:06:39,078 --> 01:06:43,333
What will it be for you?
The latest copy of <i>The Insensate?</i>

457
01:06:43,500 --> 01:06:45,293
Revised edition of the <i>Manifesto?</i>

458
01:06:45,418 --> 01:06:49,756
Erroll Partridge.
What do you know about him?

459
01:06:49,923 --> 01:06:52,467
- I beg your pardon?
- Everyone out now.

460
01:07:06,523 --> 01:07:08,483
I'm gonna ask you one more time.

461
01:07:08,650 --> 01:07:11,194
Erroll Partridge.
What do you know about him?

462
01:07:11,361 --> 01:07:14,364
I'm sure this must be
some mistake.

463
01:07:14,489 --> 01:07:16,199
- You're an offender.
- I'm not.

464
01:07:16,366 --> 01:07:19,327
No? Then why are you
so scared of me?

465
01:07:19,452 --> 01:07:22,622
You will tell me everything
you know about Erroll Partridge,

466
01:07:22,747 --> 01:07:25,542
or I will have you taken
to the Hall of Destruction

467
01:07:25,708 --> 01:07:28,044
for summary combustion.

468
01:07:28,211 --> 01:07:31,589
- I really don't know...
- Speak!

469
01:07:31,714 --> 01:07:34,509
He'd come in here
with a fella named Jurgen.

470
01:07:34,676 --> 01:07:38,513
- Why?
- That's all I know, I swear.

471
01:08:00,743 --> 01:08:02,787
Interesting.

472
01:08:40,575 --> 01:08:43,494
We've been watching you, Preston.

473
01:08:50,209 --> 01:08:52,754
You're Jurgen.

474
01:08:52,920 --> 01:08:55,298
You're feeling.

475
01:09:10,104 --> 01:09:11,773
Do you know why you came?

476
01:09:34,921 --> 01:09:37,006
Welcome to the Underground.

477
01:09:48,101 --> 01:09:50,186
Polygraph.

478
01:09:50,353 --> 01:09:53,022
It detects fluctuations
of human emotion.

479
01:09:54,399 --> 01:09:56,776
We have to be sure.

480
01:10:01,531 --> 01:10:02,615
Mary.

481
01:10:09,330 --> 01:10:13,584
You're carrying in your pocket
a ribbon sprayed with her scent.

482
01:10:16,421 --> 01:10:21,217
You breathe it in sometimes when
you think there's no one to see.

483
01:10:21,342 --> 01:10:23,428
But what you feel...

484
01:10:25,430 --> 01:10:30,059
could only be satisfied
by folding yourself into her.

485
01:10:36,607 --> 01:10:38,901
She's scheduled for combustion.

486
01:10:39,777 --> 01:10:41,863
Tomorrow.

487
01:10:47,243 --> 01:10:49,162
I know.

488
01:10:50,830 --> 01:10:53,750
You know, I was like you.

489
01:10:53,916 --> 01:10:56,794
But the first thing you learn
about emotion

490
01:10:56,919 --> 01:10:59,922
is that it has its price.

491
01:11:00,089 --> 01:11:02,842
A complete paradox.

492
01:11:02,967 --> 01:11:08,181
But without restraint,
without control,

493
01:11:08,306 --> 01:11:10,933
emotion is chaos.

494
01:11:11,058 --> 01:11:13,227
- How is that...
- The difference is that

495
01:11:13,352 --> 01:11:17,440
when we want to feel, we can.

496
01:11:18,941 --> 01:11:22,820
It's just that... some of us...

497
01:11:25,656 --> 01:11:30,953
have to forego that luxury
so that the rest can have it.

498
01:11:31,078 --> 01:11:35,875
Some very few of us have
to force ourselves not to feel.

499
01:11:38,878 --> 01:11:41,672
Like me.

500
01:11:41,798 --> 01:11:43,883
Like you.

501
01:11:48,971 --> 01:11:50,556
What can I do?

502
01:11:53,142 --> 01:11:55,186
You can kill Father.

503
01:12:23,130 --> 01:12:24,423
[truck horn beeps]

504
01:12:30,638 --> 01:12:35,351
Cleric John Preston,
you are to come with us immediately.

505
01:12:46,070 --> 01:12:48,573
- Cleric Preston.
- Sir.

506
01:12:51,284 --> 01:12:54,620
I've heard
the most disturbing rumour.

507
01:12:54,745 --> 01:12:56,998
- Rumour, sir?
- Yes.

508
01:12:57,123 --> 01:13:00,710
A rumour maintaining
that one of us, one of the Cleric,

509
01:13:00,877 --> 01:13:04,755
has secretly taken it
upon himself to cease his dose.

510
